## Connecting to a Backend

The Highstate CLI can inspect and manage the same active backend used by the Designer.
It connects through the public API, so the backend must be running and the API key must have permission to
access the requested project.

A **context** gives a backend URL a local name and can remember one selected project.
The CLI stores context metadata in your user configuration directory and stores the API token in your operating
system keyring.
It checks keyring access before asking for the token.
If the keyring is unavailable, interactive use can choose to store the token in plaintext in the context file;
the confirmation defaults to no.
Pass `--insecure` to select plaintext storage directly, including in non-interactive environments.

Create a context interactively and follow the prompts for the backend URL and API token:

```bash
highstate context add production
```

For non-interactive use, provide the URL and read the token from standard input:

```bash
printf '%s' "$HIGHSTATE_API_TOKEN" | highstate context add production \
  --api-url https://api.highstate.example.com \
  --api-token-stdin
```

The first context becomes active automatically.
You can add more backends and switch between them:

```bash
highstate context list
highstate context use production
highstate context test
```

List the projects visible to the API key, then select one for later commands:

```bash
highstate project list
highstate project use <project-id>
```

The project must be unlocked before the public API can access its model or runtime state.

## Agent Integration

Highstate provides a small installable skill that discovers version-aligned reference documents from the CLI.
Install it with the [Skills CLI](https://skills.sh):

```bash
npx skills add highstate-io/skills --skill highstate
```

## Selecting a Target

Most API commands accept `--context`, `--api-url`, and `--project`.
Explicit flags take precedence over environment variables, and environment variables take precedence over the
active context.

The supported environment variables are:

```text
HIGHSTATE_CONTEXT
HIGHSTATE_API_URL
HIGHSTATE_API_TOKEN
HIGHSTATE_PROJECT_ID
```

This makes a context convenient for interactive work while allowing an automated process to select every target
explicitly:

```bash
HIGHSTATE_API_URL=https://api.highstate.example.com \
HIGHSTATE_API_TOKEN="$TOKEN" \
HIGHSTATE_PROJECT_ID="$PROJECT_ID" \
highstate instance list --output json
```

## Inspecting a Project

Start with compact listings, then request complete objects only when you need them:

```bash
highstate library list
highstate component get common.existing-server.v1
highstate component schema common.existing-server.v1

highstate instance list
highstate instance get common.existing-server.v1:server --with-state
highstate state list
highstate operation list
```

`instance get --recursive` includes evaluated composite children and retained ghost children.
`state get --all-projections` includes evaluation, operation, parent, extra, and custom-status projections.

Use `--all` on paginated list commands when you need every page.
Without it, the response includes the backend's continuation token for the next request.

## Structured Output

Commands use a kubectl-style human-readable format by default.
List commands print aligned tables with headers, get commands print labeled resource details, and mutation
commands print a concise result.
Use `--output json` or `--output yaml` for scripts:

```bash
highstate project get --output json
highstate model get --include-virtual --include-ghost --output yaml
```

Structured output uses the public API's snake-case field names.
Normal results are written to standard output and diagnostics are written to standard error, so standard output
can be parsed without removing log lines.

## Editing the Model

Complex model values are provided as JSON or YAML documents.
Pass `-` as the path to read a document from standard input.

Create one instance:

```bash
highstate instance create --file - <<'YAML'
id: common.existing-server.v1:server
kind: unit
type: common.existing-server.v1
name: server
args:
  hostname: server.example.com
position:
  x: 100
  y: 100
YAML
```

Patch only selected fields:

```bash
highstate instance update common.existing-server.v1:server --patch - <<'YAML'
args:
  hostname: replacement.example.com
YAML
```

The keys present in a patch form the update mask.
An omitted field remains unchanged, an empty collection clears that collection, and `position: null` clears the
canvas position.

For nested argument changes, use `instance args` instead of replacing the complete argument map:

```bash
highstate instance args get common.existing-server.v1:server
highstate instance args patch common.existing-server.v1:server \
  --set /hostname=replacement.example.com \
  --set-string /labels/environment=production \
  --unset /obsolete
```

Paths are [JSON Pointers](https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/html/rfc6901) rooted at the argument map.
`--set` parses its value as YAML, while `--set-string` always uses the text after `=` as a string.
`--set-file /path=<file>` reads a JSON or YAML value from a file, and `--dry-run` validates the result without
saving it.

Use `--patch` for ordered `add`, `replace`, `remove`, and `test` operations:

```yaml [arguments.patch.yaml]
- op: test
  path: /hostname
  value: server.example.com
- op: replace
  path: /hostname
  value: replacement.example.com
- op: add
  path: /ports/-
  value: 443
```

```bash
highstate instance args patch common.existing-server.v1:server \
  --patch arguments.patch.yaml
```

All operations and final schema validation succeed or no change is saved.
YAML argument wrappers are traversed transparently, but ordinary string values remain opaque.

`highstate model create --file <path>` creates multiple instances and hubs atomically.
The document has top-level `instances` and `hubs` arrays.

Deleting an instance removes it from desired state but does not automatically destroy deployed infrastructure.
Run and complete the required destroy operation before deleting the model object.
Deletion and cancellation commands require `--yes`.

## Planning Operations

Infrastructure operations support `update`, `preview`, `destroy`, `recreate`, and `refresh`.
Plan an operation and save the exact reviewed plan:

```bash
highstate operation plan update common.existing-server.v1:server \
  --output json > plan.json
```

Review `plan.json`, then launch those phases without replanning:

```bash
highstate operation launch \
  --plan plan.json \
  --title "Update server"
```

The plan document records its format version, project, operation type, requested instances, options, and phases.
The CLI rejects a plan when its project differs from the selected project.

For interactive use, `operation run` combines planning, review, confirmation, and launch:

```bash
highstate operation run update common.existing-server.v1:server
```

Non-interactive execution requires `--yes`:

```bash
highstate operation run update common.existing-server.v1:server \
  --yes \
  --output json
```

Observe or control a launched operation with:

```bash
highstate operation get <operation-id>
highstate operation logs <operation-id> --follow
highstate operation wait <operation-id> --timeout 600
highstate operation cancel <operation-id> --yes
highstate operation cancel-instance <operation-id> <instance-id> --yes
```

The first cancellation request asks the active operation or instance to stop gracefully.
If graceful cancellation is already in progress, the command suggests adding `--force` instead of escalating
automatically.
Run the same command with `--force` to request hard cancellation.
Hard cancellation can interrupt cleanup and leave partial infrastructure changes.

`--timeout` is measured in seconds.
